    "Here are the improvements I'd make to the tier system:\n\n---\n\n**1. Dynamic tier weighting based on time of day**\n\nRight now tiers are static. But news consumption patterns change:\n- Morning (6–9 AM): people want hard news — Tier 1 should dominate\n- Midday (12–2 PM): mixed — Tier 1 + Tier 2\n- Evening (6–10 PM): people relax — Tier 3 gets more weight\n\nSo \"Entertainment\" at 8 PM should compete closer to \"Politics\" than it does at 8 AM. The multiplier should shift by time window, not be a fixed value.\n\n---\n\n**2. Story-level tier override — a story can punch above its category**\n\nA category is just a default tier. But individual stories within a category can be exceptional:\n- A celebrity death → Entertainment story but Tier 1 urgency\n- A jobs report showing 500,000 layoffs → Jobs category but Tier 1 impact\n- A sports match-fixing scandal → Sports but Tier 1 crime significance\n\nThe scorer should detect these exceptions and promote the story's effective tier regardless of its category.\n\n---\n\n**3. Tier 3 surge allowance on breaking news days**\n\nOn days when Tier 1 has 8+ stories published, the audience needs a break from heavy news. Tier 3 stories should automatically get a temporary score boost to provide relief content between hard news posts. Right now Tier 3 is always suppressed even when Tier 1 is exhausted.\n\n---\n\n**4. Per-category daily slot allocation**\n\nInstead of a flat 10-post daily cap, allocate slots by tier:\n```\nTier 1 — up to 6 slots/day (but minimum 0 — don't force it)\nTier 2 — up to 3 slots/day\nTier 3 — up to 2 slots/day\n```\nThis prevents a single category flood (e.g. 8 war stories in a row) while still allowing big news days.\n\n---\n\n**5. Audience fatigue detection**\n\nIf 3+ consecutive published posts are from the same tier (e.g. 3 war stories back to back), the next slot should be forced to a different tier — even if the queue has more Tier 1 stories waiting. People disengage from endless heavy news.\n\n---\n\n**6. Weekend/weekday mode**\n\nSports and Entertainment naturally perform better on weekends. Jobs and Business perform better on weekdays. The tier multipliers should shift automatically based on day of week.\n\n---\n\n**7. Feedback loop from Facebook engagement**\n\nLong term: track which posts got the most reactions/comments/shares via the Graph API insights endpoint. If War stories consistently underperform while Science stories overperform for your specific audience, the tier weights should adjust automatically over time.\n\n---\n\nWant me to implement any of these? The most impactful ones to build first would be **#2 (story-level override)**, **#4 (per-category slot allocation)**, and **#5 (audience fatigue detection)** — those three together would make the system genuinely intelligent rather than just rule-based.\n[Tool uses: none]",
