    "Here's what's missing from the story lifecycle right now:\n\n---\n\n**1. Story aging & re-evaluation**\n\nA story sitting in the HOLD queue for 2 hours should be re-scored before publishing. What was a 65 (HOLD) at 8 AM might now be a 48 (REJECT) at 10 AM because it's stale. Right now HOLD stories are retried at their original score forever.\n\n---\n\n**2. Post-publish performance tracking**\n\nAfter publishing, we never go back to check how the post performed. The Graph API gives you reactions, comments, shares, and reach per post. If we log this back against the story's category, source, and score — over time you build a real signal about what your specific audience responds to. Right now the scoring is entirely editorial opinion with no feedback loop.\n\n---\n\n**3. Story clustering — related stories become one post**\n\nIf 4 sources all publish about the same event (US-Iran strikes), right now they compete individually in the queue. A better approach: cluster them into one story object with the strongest headline, all corroborating sources merged in, and the freshest facts. One authoritative post beats four thin ones.\n\n---\n\n**4. Follow-up detection**\n\n\"Ukraine ceasefire talks begin\" published at 9 AM. At 2 PM: \"Ukraine ceasefire talks collapse\". The system has no awareness that the second story is a follow-up to something already published. A follow-up should be prioritised and its post should reference the earlier one (\"Earlier today we reported...\").\n\n---\n\n**5. Story retirement**\n\nOnce a story is published, related stories on the same event should be automatically suppressed for 4–6 hours — not just deduplicated by URL, but by topic. Right now two slightly different angles on the same event can both pass dedup and both get published.\n\n---\n\n**6. Draft review mode**\n\nBefore full automation was turned on, there was a human review step. That's gone now. There's no middle ground — it's either fully automated or nothing. A \"review queue\" that flags borderline stories (score 60–72) for optional human approval before publishing would give you a safety net on weak days.\n\n---\n\n**7. Story enrichment**\n\nWhen a story passes the DNP gate, it only has title + summary + source. Before writing the post, we could enrich it: pull the full article text from the URL, extract key quotes, identify named entities (people, places, organisations). The AI post writer would produce significantly better output with this context instead of working from a 2-sentence RSS summary.\n\n---\n\n**8. Breaking news alert mode**\n\nIf a Tier 1 story scores 90+ and is under 30 minutes old, it should bypass the normal scheduler completely and publish within 5 minutes. Right now even 90+ stories wait for the next scheduler slot (07:30 / 12:30 / 19:30). Breaking news needs to be near-real-time.\n\n---\n\nThe three I'd build first:\n\n- **#3 (story clustering)** — biggest quality improvement, stops flooding the page with the same event from different angles\n- **#4 (follow-up detection)** — makes the page feel like a live news feed, not a collection of disconnected stories\n- **#8 (breaking news alert mode)** — real news pages don't wait 7 hours to post a 90-point breaking story\n\nWant me to implement any of these?\n[Tool uses: none]",
