    "The current AI check has 3 real weaknesses worth fixing:\n\n---\n\n**Weakness 1 — The LLM can't actually see the image**\n\nRight now we describe the card in text and ask Ollama to score \"visual impact\" and \"brand consistency\" — but it's scoring based on the headline and category name, not the actual pixel output. It's essentially guessing.\n\nThe fix: encode the saved image as base64 and pass it as a vision input. Ollama's llama3.2 supports vision if you use the multimodal variant (`llama3.2-vision`). If not available, use the composed card's pixel data to extract concrete facts (dominant colors, brightness zones) and inject those as structured context into the prompt instead of vague descriptions. This way even a text model scores against real card data.\n\n**This is the most impactful improvement.**\n\n---\n\n**Weakness 2 — One prompt asking 7 questions gets lazy answers**\n\nWhen you ask an LLM to score 7 criteria in one shot, it tends to cluster scores around a similar range — usually 13-16 on everything — because it's pattern-matching \"this looks like a reasonable news post\" rather than thinking carefully about each dimension. The scores end up not meaningfully differentiated.\n\nThe fix: chain-of-thought scoring. Add a `\"reasoning\"` field to the JSON output where the model must justify each score with one specific observation before writing the number. This forces it to actually evaluate each criterion rather than output a default distribution.\n\nExample prompt addition:\n```\nBefore scoring each criterion, state ONE specific observation about the post \nthat justifies your score. Then give the number.\n```\n\n---\n\n**Weakness 3 — No feedback loop — scores are never checked against reality**\n\nThe agent scores cards but never learns whether high-scoring cards actually performed better on Facebook. Over time, the scoring becomes decoupled from real audience behavior.\n\nThe fix: log the attention score alongside the Facebook post ID in a `score